# Disable systemd-oomd on steelhead (2026-09-26, device r110).
#
# It was the single largest CPU consumer on an idle Q: 10 ms of work every
# 1.25 s, 0.81 % of a core, measured by a passive sched trace on the cottage unit
# overnight — more than nq-healthd, the whole telemetry loop. And it guards
# against nothing this appliance meets: a fixed set of services on 1 GB, whose
# MemAvailable never went below 849 MiB over the night, swap untouched. The
# kernel's own OOM killer remains the backstop, as on every Linux box; oomd was
# only ever a userspace layer in front of it. oomd was also the only consumer of
# /proc/pressure (CONFIG_PSI, kernel 6.18.48-r1); whether PSI's own averaging
# work is worth keeping without it is measured separately, not assumed.
#
# 90-systemd.preset ships `enable systemd-oomd.service` (whose Also= pulls in the
# socket); systemctl preset resolves each unit against the FIRST matching rule in
# lexicographic filename order, so this file must sort before 90-* — hence 20-,
# like 20-nexusq-nsresourced-off.preset. `disable`, not a /dev/null mask, for the
# same reason as there: a masked system unit makes preset-all complain.
#
# A preset only decides what the NEXT preset pass does. A fresh image gets the
# enable symlinks removed by .post-install; a box already in the field gets
# `systemctl disable --now` from .post-upgrade. And none of that is enough by
# itself: systemd's own 10-oomd-defaults.conf drop-ins give user@.service and the
# root/system slices ManagedOOMMemoryPressure=kill, which PID 1 turns into an
# implicit Wants=systemd-oomd.service. The package shadows those drop-ins with
# /dev/null in /etc/systemd/system/ (see the APKBUILD).
disable systemd-oomd.service
disable systemd-oomd.socket
